      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "contradictory",
      "decision_effect": "weakened",
      "card_ids": ["C20", "C23", "C33", "C50", "C63"]
    },
    {
      "family": "associated_nonvoting_records",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C37", "C61", "C38", "C42", "C46", "C48", "C51", "C24", "C30", "C40", "C49", "C31", "C56"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct measured absolute oral bioavailability for the query molecule is provided.",
    "The low-F inference relies on structural analogy to naloxone/naloxegol and pentazocine controls rather than a direct query measurement.",
    "No query-specific solubility, permeability, efflux, or first-pass metabolism data are supplied.",
    "Healthy-subject F for meptazinol is not provided; only the cirrhosis value is available.",
    "Transfer from pentazocine (benzomorphan) to the 4,5-epoxymorphinan query is indirect."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Flipped to low: new L2 evidence shows the high F values in cirrhosis are due to reduced first-pass metabolism (pentazocine control F=0.18; pethidine normal 0.48), and C36 indicates the unPEGylated 4,5-epoxymorphinan with 6-keto/14-hydroxy (naloxone-like) has poor oral bioavailability relative to PEGylated naloxegol; codeine's high F is less transferable because it lacks these metabolic liabilities. Thus F < 20% is better supported."
}

